   "name": "eCFR, 49 CFR 71.7(e) and 71.8 (Central/Mountain zone boundary), current text retrieved 2026-10-26",
   "url": "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-A/part-71/section-71.7",
   "tier": "PRIMARY_OFFICIAL"
  },
  "rule": "The central/mountain boundary follows the west boundary of Texas to the southeast corner of New Mexico, then west along the Texas-New Mexico line to the EAST line of Hudspeth County, then south along the east line of Hudspeth County to the Mexico border. Counties west of that line (El Paso, Hudspeth) are Mountain; every other Texas county is Central.",
  "pollClose": "7:00 p.m. local time in each county (Tex. Elec. Code 41.031, per Texas SOS Election Advisory 2026-21). Local clock time is not converted here to a statewide instant.",
  "dstOnElectionDay": "None. Daylight time ends 2026-11-01, so on 2026-11-03 Central is UTC-6 and Mountain is UTC-7.",
  "utcOffsetHours": {
   "CENTRAL": -6,
   "MOUNTAIN": -7
  },
  "limitations": [
   "Legal zone only. A county seat or park that informally follows a neighbouring zone (for example reports about the Guadalupe Mountains National Park in Culberson County) is not a legal exception and was not verified.",
   "The CFR describes a boundary, not a county list. Membership was derived by geography (El Paso lies west of Hudspeth) and should be confirmed by a second source before it feeds anything beyond ordering of poll-close times.",
   "Whether the Texas Secretary of State or any county publishes an alternative closing time for Nov 3 was not checked."
  ],
  "counties": 254
